  "query_preview": "../Thursday through Sunday/...Thursday starts with \nlingering showers in the northern panhandle and increasing cloud \ncover from north to south, as a front in the northern gulf moves \neastward into Yakutat that morning. The front will continue to \npush inland into the northern panhandle, spreading to the central \npanhandle through the night and reaching the southern panhandle \nthrough Friday morning. Increased PoPs slightly as the front \ninitially moves through, particularly for the outer coast. Rain \nwill start to taper off from north to south through Friday and \ninto Saturday, though potential for showers will keep PoPs \nelevated through the weekend. \n\nRainfall rates will remain relatively light through this period, \nespecially behind the initial front, though more developed showers\nmay bring periods of heavier rainfall and gusty winds as they \nblow over. Winds will increase for parts of the N/S facing inner \nchannels with the front, though expecting speeds to remain around \n15 to 20 mph and decrease into the weekend. The strongest speeds \nare expected through Northern Lynn Canal and into Taiya Inlet, \nwhich may be accentuated by any light sea breezing through the \nafternoon.\n\nAs this low dips southward through the weekend, easterly flow \nindicates potential for Thunderstorms in BC to attempt to make it \nover the Coastal Range and into the panhandle. While it is still \ntoo far out to decide if this will be a strong enough pattern, \nthis will continue to be looked at through the week. High \ntemperatures will be capped around the low to mid 60s with the \nincreased cloud cover and active weather in the forecast.",
